WEBVTT

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.530
Our heart pumps blood through an extensive network of blood vessels.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.170
This is how oxygen and nutrients reach our cells.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:12.770
At the same time, blood collects waste products and carries them away.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:15.770
Arteries carry blood away from the heart.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.600
In tiny capillaries, substances are exchanged between blood and tissue.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.500
Veins bring blood back to the heart.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:27.550
Red and blue help us distinguish different blood vessels.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.740
But our blood is always red.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.180
Endovascular means: inside a blood vessel.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.620
A catheter is a thin, flexible tube.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.870
Doctors carefully guide it through the inside of a blood vessel.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.080
They track its position using medical imaging.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.500
We show one possible route to the heart.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.570
The catheter starts in a vein in the groin.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.740
It advances through the pelvic veins into the inferior vena cava.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:02.760
This large vein returns blood from the lower body to the heart.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.780
From there, the catheter tip reaches the right atrium.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:09.670
That is one of the heart’s two upper chambers.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:13.950
Depending on the examination, a catheter can measure pressure, for example.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:18.560
Other procedures use arteries and therefore follow a different route.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:24.020
At our station, you explore this principle using a simplified model.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.430
You control it from outside and watch the tip.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:32.920
The challenge is to find the right route and control your movements precisely.
